<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orisha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as orishas, òrìṣà, orixá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The orishas are the divine spirits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and of the religions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> that derive from it, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a>, Cuban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a> and Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a>. According to these traditions they are spirits sent by the supreme creator, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a>, to help humanity on Earth, and many are held to have lived first in the spirit realm and then as humans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> carried their worship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. In the Lucumí tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> the orishas were identified with Catholic saints, which let enslaved Africans keep their faith under colonial persecution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orisha.jpg" alt="Orisha" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Turismo Bahia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dique_do_Tororó_-_Foto_-_Tereza_Torres_-_Setur_(15659893633).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ife is called the city of 401 deities: the Ooni is counted the 401st <b>orisha</b>, the only one that speaks, and traditional worshippers are said to celebrate a festival of one deity on every day of the year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…sacred grove of the river goddess Oshun at Osogbo in Nigeria, one of the last stretches of primary high forest in southern Nigeria, was restored with the help of the Austrian artist Susanne Wenger, who became a priestess of the <b>orisha</b>, and is now a World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
